s://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5334504?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/saturninoabril/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/saturninoabril/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/saturninoabril/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/saturninoabril/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/saturninoabril","id":5334504,"login":"saturninoabril","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUzMzQ1MDQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/saturninoabril/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/saturninoabril/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/saturninoabril/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/saturninoabril/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/saturninoabril/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/saturninoabril","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/engage-chat/mattermost/commits/7a51ead546b89e62c91dddb8c538d5a2e843ce85/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-08T05:00:57Z","email":"5334504+saturninoabril@users.noreply.github.com","name":"sabril"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-08T05:00:57Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"Automated cherry pick of #38253 and #38261 (#38394)\n\n* Hand Cypress E2E deps to workers as a run-scoped artifact (#38253)\n\nprep-deps wrote node_modules + the Cypress binary to a shared Actions\ncache keyed on the cypress lockfile, and the 40 workers read it back\nwithin the same run.\n\nThe repo is pinned at the 10 GB Actions cache ceiling (100 entries,\n9.99 GB), so LRU eviction runs continuously. Every distinct cypress\nlockfile a run happens to build costs a ~275 MB entry in that budget —\nfour are live today, 1.10 GB total — and they evict entries other jobs\ndepend on: the webapp node_modules cache (3.7 GB), the Playwright deps\ncache, and the E2E npm registry cache.\n\nBuild the deps once in prep-deps and upload them as a run-scoped\nartifact instead. The handoff is unchanged in shape — one producer, N\nconsumers, within a run — but it costs the shared cache nothing, and it\ndrops the fail-on-cache-miss cliff the workers previously depended on.\n\nThe artifact name is scoped by edition: e2e-tests-ci.yml invokes this\ntemplate twice per workflow run (default and fips) and artifact names\nare run-scoped, so the two invocations would otherwise collide.\n\nprep-deps loses its cache-hit shortcut and now always installs, so it\nrestores the master-warmed ~/.npm registry cache read-only to keep that\ninstall short, and its timeout goes 10 -> 15 minutes.\n\nPlaywright still uses the cache handoff; it is a larger payload with a\nchurnier key and follows separately.\n\nCo-authored-by: saturnino <saturnino@mattermost.com>\n\n* Warm the Cypress deps cache from master and prefer it over the artifact (#38261)\n\n* Warm the Cypress deps cache from master and prefer it over the artifact\n\nThe parent commit hands the Cypress deps to the workers as a run-scoped\nartifact, which costs the shared Actions cache nothing but makes\nprep-deps install on every run. A baseline run measured the cache path\nit replaced at 6s per worker with prep-deps skipping the install\nentirely, so paying an install plus a ~275 MB upload on every run is a\npoor trade for the common case.\n\nRestore the fast path without restoring the churn. A new scheduled\nworkflow becomes the sole writer of the deps key, warming it from master\ndaily and on any push that changes the cypress lockfile. prep-deps then\nprobes the key with lookup-only — a metadata call, not a download — and\npublishes the result as a job output. Workers restore the warmed entry\non a hit and unpack the artifact only on a miss, so exactly one of the\ntwo paths runs.\n\nThe net effect on the cache budget is one warm entry per master lockfile\ninstead of one per lockfile any run happens to build; the four live\nentries today total 1.10 GB. PR and merge runs still never write.\n\nrestore-cypress-deps centralises the key so the warm workflow, the probe\nand the workers cannot drift apart. Its payload must stay identical to\npack/unpack-cypress-deps, since the cache entry and the artifact are\ninterchangeable by construction.\n\nA separate binary-only cache is deliberately omitted: keyed on the same\nlockfile hash, it would invalidate in lockstep with the deps key and\nearn its keep only when one entry is evicted and the other survives.\nKeying it on the resolved cypress version instead would be a real win\nand can follow on its own.\n\n* Spell out which transport each Cypress deps action provides\n\nrestore-cypress-deps and unpack-cypress-deps both leave a worker with\nnode_modules and the Cypress binary, differing only in where they fetch\nthem from, and nothing in their descriptions said so. Name the transport\neach one implements, when it is the one that runs, and the invariant that\nlets the workers accept either.\n\nNo behaviour change; description metadata only.\n\n* Warm the cache only from master, even on manual dispatch\n\n* Report the Cypress deps cache outcome to the job summary\n\nWhich transport a run took was only legible by opening prep-deps and\nreading which steps the probe skipped, and whether the warm workflow\nactually saved anything was just as buried. Report both to\nGITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY: prep-deps prints hit or miss, and the warm job\nprints saved or already warm.\n\nBoth print the computed key alongside the verdict, which is what makes a\nmiss diagnosable — a lockfile or path drift reads as an unexpected key\nrather than an unexplained miss.\n\nThe workers are deliberately left alone. Their summary would be twenty\nnear-identical lines per run, and the hit path already runs with\nfail-on-cache-miss, so a worker either restores or fails loudly.\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Jesse Hallam <jesse.hallam@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: saturnino <saturnino@mattermost.com>","tree":{"sha":"3fd4d56af75977b0fc9709064eeab8062700409c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/engage-chat/mattermost/git/trees/3fd4d56af75977b0fc9709064eeab8062700409c"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/engage-chat/mattermost/git/commits/7a51ead546b89e62c91dddb8c538d5a2e843ce85","verification":{"payload":"tree 3fd4d56af75977b0fc9709064eeab8062700409c\nparent 981ff7f7a9747726c92c3963fec28523da0fe350\nauthor sabril <5334504+saturninoabril@users.noreply.github.com> 1788843657 +0800\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788843657 +0800\n\nAutomated cherry pick of #38253 and #38261 (#38394)\n\n* Hand Cypress E2E deps to workers as a run-scoped artifact (#38253)\n\nprep-deps wrote node_modules + the Cypress binary to a shared Actions\ncache keyed on the cypress lockfile, and the 40 workers read it back\nwithin the same run.\n\nThe repo is pinned at the 10 GB Actions cache ceiling (100 entries,\n9.99 GB), so LRU eviction runs continuously. Every distinct cypress\nlockfile a run happens to build costs a ~275 MB entry in that budget —\nfour are live today, 1.10 GB total — and they evict entries other jobs\ndepend on: the webapp node_modules cache (3.7 GB), the Playwright deps\ncache, and the E2E npm registry cache.\n\nBuild the deps once in prep-deps and upload them as a run-scoped\nartifact instead. The handoff is unchanged in shape — one producer, N\nconsumers, within a run — but it costs the shared cache nothing, and it\ndrops the fail-on-cache-miss cliff the workers previously depended on.\n\nThe artifact name is scoped by edition: e2e-tests-ci.yml invokes this\ntemplate twice per workflow run (default and fips) and artifact names\nare run-scoped, so the two invocations would otherwise collide.\n\nprep-deps loses its cache-hit shortcut and now always installs, so it\nrestores the master-warmed ~/.npm registry cache read-only to keep that\ninstall short, and its timeout goes 10 -> 15 minutes.\n\nPlaywright still uses the cache handoff; it is a larger payload with a\nchurnier key and follows separately.\n\nCo-authored-by: saturnino <saturnino@mattermost.com>\n\n* Warm the Cypress deps cache from master and prefer it over the artifact (#38261)\n\n* Warm the Cypress deps cache from master and prefer it over the artifact\n\nThe parent commit hands the Cypress deps to the workers as a run-scoped\nartifact, which costs the shared Actions cache nothing but makes\nprep-deps install on every run. A baseline run measured the cache path\nit replaced at 6s per worker with prep-deps skipping the install\nentirely, so paying an install plus a ~275 MB upload on every run is a\npoor trade for the common case.\n\nRestore the fast path without restoring the churn. A new scheduled\nworkflow becomes the sole writer of the deps key, warming it from master\ndaily and on any push that changes the cypress lockfile. prep-deps then\nprobes the key with lookup-only — a metadata call, not a download — and\npublishes the result as a job output. Workers restore the warmed entry\non a hit and unpack the artifact only on a miss, so exactly one of the\ntwo paths runs.\n\nThe net effect on the cache budget is one warm entry per master lockfile\ninstead of one per lockfile any run happens to build; the four live\nentries today total 1.10 GB. PR and merge runs still never write.\n\nrestore-cypress-deps centralises the key so the warm workflow, the probe\nand the workers cannot drift apart. Its payload must stay identical to\npack/unpack-cypress-deps, since the cache entry and the artifact are\ninterchangeable by construction.\n\nA separate binary-only cache is deliberately omitted: keyed on the same\nlockfile hash, it would invalidate in lockstep with the deps key and\nearn its keep only when one entry is evicted and the other survives.\nKeying it on the resolved cypress version instead would be a real win\nand can follow on its own.\n\n* Spell out which transport each Cypress deps action provides\n\nrestore-cypress-deps and unpack-cypress-deps both leave a worker with\nnode_modules and the Cypress binary, differing only in where they fetch\nthem from, and nothing in their descriptions said so. Name the transport\neach one implements, when it is the one that runs, and the invariant that\nlets the workers accept either.\n\nNo behaviour change; description metadata only.\n\n* Warm the cache only from master, even on manual dispatch\n\n* Report the Cypress deps cache outcome to the job summary\n\nWhich transport a run took was only legible by opening prep-deps and\nreading which steps the probe skipped, and whether the warm workflow\nactually saved anything was just as buried. Report both to\nGITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY: prep-deps prints hit or miss, and the warm job\nprints saved or already warm.\n\nBoth print the computed key alongside the verdict, which is what makes a\nmiss diagnosable — a lockfile or path drift reads as an unexpected key\nrather than an unexplained miss.\n\nThe workers are deliberately left alone. Their summary would be twenty\nnear-identical lines per run, and the hit path already runs with\nfail-on-cache-miss, so a worker either restores or fails loudly.\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Jesse Hallam <jesse.hallam@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: saturnino <saturnino@mattermost.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqn5aJCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAJ2QQAEiKQLNfPnyvfjReu7gYL7Kx\nC4EOQPQnC47SiA2vEMg0oTUeob4i32pSFOQuAeyJ/BfXtr2mURXrZOO0JtWyUR+8\nG1Grmg16B5W5jmL/ND3YxERA+ElXX/GfkQ6f/nQvaxXygtfZaNXazsBYfy9Vcewx\n0j5s2BV4uhnsoKwsmWLIyAdxpAO3LupD9FnjiVhBbEhhCgSbH8b/CcxD3VvxYPRV\npegDguqyKxdnOXtbgzaP/jJ/cLTUXQ5w7pFVPGTx/JGQyGKW9y7wQs6SlPTts/Gl\noXj4uWG5Ze4ELa4dCm2kVLL1gUw2fVhMQJHXqj4EwBky+Uu58xoImPSScjQlxtYZ\nEV0tqJhx1/GAkVrL+5uUxaIWpWOwcfm5f/B/ePAEXMYlwG091J2slBy4wDqUaLiF\nWUtJRz1lZ7H6rn1czcWYbfi9/NiNBCgNkB0DuLG+CISGtl6UZZi8eMNFwoS95vAs\nljsGXJfXbka6eWVZATWE5OgokyO07drN80789UW65Fbm6Wla80LBw7DPhetH8LAG\n/PubghzS/pgV1LCReWgeTqaz4uZy22Z